Here's an example of automatically generating a table of contents (ToC) in a LATEX document using the `toc` package and its `\tableofcontents` command. To generate a table of contents for your document, place the command `\tableofcontents` in the desired location of your document, typically right after or before the `\begin{document}` command. This will generate a ToC automatically based on the sectioning commands used in your document (e.g., `\section`, `\subsection`, `\subsubsection`, etc.). Additionally, you may want to use the `titletoc` package to have finer control over the appearance of the ToC. Here's a minimal working example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft} % Customizing table of contents layout
\usepackage{titletoc} % Finer control over table of contents appearance
\usepackage{toc} % Table of contents generation

\begin{document}

% Add a table of contents (ToC) using the 'toc' package
\tableofcontents

\section{Section Title}
This is the content of the first section.

\subsection{Subsection Title}
This is the content of the first subsection.

\subsubsection{Subsubsection Title}
This is the content of the first subsubsection.

% Customize the ToC using the 'titletoc' package
\titlecontents{section}[0em] % Customize section ToC entries
{\addvspace{.5em}}           % Add a vertical space before section entries
{\hspace*{-0.5em}\color{red}\thecontentslabel} % Print the section number in red
{}
{\;\;\;\;\;}                 % Add two emspaces after the entry
[\hfill\contentspage]         % Add the page number on the right

\titlecontents{subsection}[1.5em]
{\addvspace{0.25em}}
{\thecontentslabel}
{}
{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\,}
[{\color{blue}\thecontentspage}]

\titlecontents{subsubsection}[3.0em]
