Here's a C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

int countWordOccurences(const string& word, const string& filePath) {
  int wordCount = 0;

  // Open the text file
  ifstream file(filePath);
  if (!file.is_open()) {
    cout << "Error opening file." << endl;
    return -1;
  }

  // Read the file line by line and count occurrences of the word
  string line;
  while (getline(file, line)) {
    size_t pos = line.find(word);
    while (pos != string::npos) {
      ++wordCount;
      pos = line.find(word, pos + word.length());
    }
  }

  // Close the file
  file.close();

  return wordCount;
}

int main() {
  const string WORD = "example"; // Replace this with the specific word you want to count
  const string FILE_PATH = "path/to/your/text/file.txt"; // Replace this with the path to your text file

  int wordOccurrences = countWordOccurences(WORD, FILE_PATH);
  cout << "The word '" << WORD << "' occurs " << wordOccurrences << " times in the text file." << endl;

  return 0;
}

This program uses the `fstream` library to open and read the text file line by line. It searches for the specified word within each line using the `find()` function, and increments the word count whenever the word is found. The number of occurrences is returned at the end of the program.

Make sure to replace the `<path/to/your/text/file.txt>` portion with the actual path to your text file, and the `WORD` constant with the specific word you wish to count occurrences for.

You can implement a queue data structure in Python using two stacks by taking advantage of each stack's Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) behavior. By alternating between the stacks when enqueuing and dequeuing elements, the order of the queue can be maintained. Here's a sample implementation of a queue using two stacks in Python:

```python
class QueueUsingTwoStacks:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack_a = []
        self.stack_b = []

    def enqueue(self, item):
        self.stack_a.append(item)

    def dequeue(self):
        # If stack_b is empty, pop all elements from stack_a and push into stack_b
        if not self.stack_b:
            while self.stack_a:
                self.stack_b.append(self.stack_a.pop())
